        },
        {
          "text": "Quantity I = Quantity II or relation can’t be established"
        }
      ],
      "answer": 1,
      "explanation": "The speed of train b = 14 m/s So, the speed of train A = 2 × 15 – 14 = 16 m/s And, the speed of train C = 16 + 4 = 20 m/s Let the lengths of trains A, B and C are ‘a’m, ‘b’m and ‘c’m respectively. a+b Now, = 13 ⇒ a + b = 390\b ... (i) 16 + 14 a+c = 100 ⇒ a + c = 400\b ... (ii) Also, 20 – 16 Since, train A can cross a 140 m long platform in 20 seconds. a + 140 = 20 ⇒ a = 180 So, From equation (i): b = 210 From equation (ii): c = 220 Quantity I: Since, original speed of train C = 20 m/s So, new speed of train C would be = 95% of 20 = 19 m/s And,…",
      "expectedTimeSec": 90,
      "difficulty": "Moderate"
